# A skills repo is mostly worked examples, so its own scan is noisy by construction. One
# Python regex per line, matched against "PATH:MATCH" by skills/repo-sensitive-scan. Every
# entry here is a documentation example, named rather than covered by a wildcard — the point
# of this file is to stay readable enough that a real finding still stands out.
#
# Note when adding to it: classify() yields the first *non-allowlisted* match per category
# per line, so silencing one match on a line can reveal the next. Re-run until the count is
# stable, and anchor on the invented name rather than on one path shape it appears in.

# `me@uni.edu`, `jdoe` and allocation `abcd` are the stand-ins the remediation recipes and
# the scanner's own docstring are written around.
:me@uni\.edu$
:/(u|home|scratch|work)/.*jdoe
:/work/nvme/abcd/
:/work/x/proj$
# `USER` in caps is a placeholder in the same docstring, but site_path() only skips $ < {.
^skills/repo-sensitive-scan/scripts/scan_sensitive\.py:/u/USER$

# Credentialed URLs quoted to explain why masking cannot key on the category. The userinfo
# is the invented half; the hosts are example.org / github.com placeholders.
:(https|scheme)://(user|admin|svc|x-access-token):(token|password|pass|Hunter2|ghp_)

# The four-part version string quoted as the known host-addr false positive.
^skills/repo-sensitive-scan/SKILL\.md:1\.2\.3\.4$

# "mason" is this repository's own name (skills_mason), in its README title.
^README\.md:mason$
